1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,809
Begin on Calls in an
authorized Responder session.

2
00:00:03,279 --> 00:00:08,350
The cards bring together a call number,
name, location and assigned resources.

3
00:00:08,739 --> 00:00:11,640
Read those details before
choosing any response.

4
00:00:12,069 --> 00:00:15,710
In this fictional department,
enter Warehouse in Search calls.

5
00:00:16,149 --> 00:00:21,379
The list narrows to TRAINING
— Warehouse alarm, numbered DEMO-0001,

6
00:00:21,690 --> 00:00:23,540
at Training site one in Chicago.

7
00:00:23,950 --> 00:00:27,540
Check the name, number and
address together; a familiar

8
00:00:27,540 --> 00:00:31,069
word in a search result is not
enough to identify an incident.

9
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:33,540
The search changes the displayed list.

10
00:00:33,900 --> 00:00:38,400
It does not rename a call, dispatch
anyone or change your personal status.

11
00:00:38,610 --> 00:00:40,849
Open that card to reach Call Details.

12
00:00:41,280 --> 00:00:45,369
The same name and number remain at the
top, followed by the nature of the call.

13
00:00:45,759 --> 00:00:50,150
Here it describes reported smoke at
an unoccupied training warehouse and

14
00:00:50,150 --> 00:00:52,089
a Squad Three search demonstration.

15
00:00:52,629 --> 00:00:55,619
The map provides context for
the stored incident location.

16
00:00:55,869 --> 00:00:59,830
It does not establish where your device
is or whether a crew has arrived.

17
00:01:00,260 --> 00:01:04,209
Route is available beside Notes,
Images and Files, but we leave

18
00:01:04,209 --> 00:01:07,260
it untouched while reviewing the
information already supplied.

19
00:01:07,440 --> 00:01:12,539
On Info, scroll until Priority,
Timestamp, Type, Address and

20
00:01:12,539 --> 00:01:14,030
Note are visible together.

21
00:01:14,440 --> 00:01:17,049
This example shows Urgent
and Structure Fire.

22
00:01:17,370 --> 00:01:21,769
Its timestamp is October twenty-fifth at
eleven forty-nine in the morning; that

23
00:01:21,769 --> 00:01:24,319
displayed time is not an arrival report.

24
00:01:24,720 --> 00:01:27,319
Check the address against
the intended training site.

25
00:01:27,700 --> 00:01:31,929
The note explicitly identifies a
fictional Academy exercise and says

26
00:01:31,929 --> 00:01:34,019
not to dispatch real resources.

27
00:01:34,370 --> 00:01:37,530
Read qualifiers like this along
with the headline, rather than

28
00:01:37,530 --> 00:01:40,640
carrying an assumption from the
call list into your next action.

29
00:01:40,770 --> 00:01:43,960
Choose Dispatched and bring all
the resource rows into view.

30
00:01:44,519 --> 00:01:49,499
Kelly Severide and Joe Cruz appear as
User entries associated with Squad Three.

31
00:01:49,930 --> 00:01:54,570
Squad Three also has its own Unit entry,
associated with Firehouse Fifty-one.

32
00:01:55,119 --> 00:01:59,869
The Group and Type labels help distinguish
a person from a vehicle or team resource.

33
00:02:00,279 --> 00:02:02,879
This is the recorded dispatch
assignment for the call.

34
00:02:03,310 --> 00:02:07,789
A name in this list does not, by itself,
prove that person acknowledged the call,

35
00:02:07,990 --> 00:02:12,400
changed status, reached the location
or received an external notification.

36
00:02:12,720 --> 00:02:15,559
Protocols and Activity
answer different questions.

37
00:02:15,930 --> 00:02:20,820
In Protocols, this call currently returns
No protocols attached to this call.

38
00:02:21,280 --> 00:02:24,499
Activity returns No
timeline events available.

39
00:02:24,999 --> 00:02:28,060
Read each result in the context
of the selected incident.

40
00:02:28,570 --> 00:02:32,439
Neither message supplies a missing
procedure or confirms an arrival.

41
00:02:32,910 --> 00:02:36,849
An empty activity view is not a
reason to invent a response history.

42
00:02:37,279 --> 00:02:40,929
If important guidance or chronology
is missing, resolve that through your

43
00:02:40,929 --> 00:02:45,159
authorized coordination process before
treating the information as complete.

44
00:02:45,330 --> 00:02:47,760
Choose Notes to inspect Call Notes.

45
00:02:48,230 --> 00:02:50,580
This example says No notes found.

46
00:02:51,019 --> 00:02:54,490
The search box can help find an
existing note when there are entries.

47
00:02:54,769 --> 00:02:59,099
Below it, Add a new note and Add
Note belong to the writing workflow.

48
00:02:59,530 --> 00:03:02,679
We leave the field blank and
use Cancel, so this inspection

49
00:03:02,720 --> 00:03:04,159
adds nothing to the incident.

50
00:03:04,540 --> 00:03:08,710
Reading the original note on Info and
checking the separate Call Notes list are

51
00:03:08,710 --> 00:03:13,460
useful complementary checks; do not assume
they are the same piece of information.

52
00:03:13,600 --> 00:03:17,050
The Images and Files buttons
open separate supporting views.

53
00:03:17,510 --> 00:03:20,650
Call Images currently
says No images available.

54
00:03:21,040 --> 00:03:24,919
Call Files says No files
available and explains that none

55
00:03:24,919 --> 00:03:26,310
have been added to this call.

56
00:03:26,700 --> 00:03:30,970
These are the results shown for this
incident, not demonstrations of uploading,

57
00:03:31,040 --> 00:03:32,929
downloading or taking a photograph.

58
00:03:33,280 --> 00:03:37,690
Check the call identity before relying
on an attachment from any source, and

59
00:03:37,690 --> 00:03:42,089
use only approved material when a later
task genuinely requires adding one.

60
00:03:42,240 --> 00:03:46,899
On Home, this example shows three
Active Calls and zero Calls I’m On.

61
00:03:47,350 --> 00:03:49,299
Those counts answer different questions.

62
00:03:49,759 --> 00:03:54,450
The Status controls include Responding,
Not Responding, On Scene and

63
00:03:54,450 --> 00:03:58,549
Standing By; the Standing By choice
remains outlined during this review.

64
00:03:58,949 --> 00:04:00,830
We do not press a response button.

65
00:04:01,399 --> 00:04:05,990
Use the same read-before-response
pattern for fire and EMS, community-care

66
00:04:05,990 --> 00:04:10,799
or mental-health teams, search and
rescue, security patrols, wildland

67
00:04:10,799 --> 00:04:12,879
contractors and industrial teams.

68
00:04:13,280 --> 00:04:17,530
Find the correct incident, read its
context and assignments, and identify

69
00:04:17,530 --> 00:04:21,550
missing information before making
an authorized operational response.

